1
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:59,513
<i>Hello. I'm Carl Burke, and today</i>
<i>we're visiting Hastings, Georgia.</i>

2
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:04,071
<i>Hastings is small-town America,</i>
<i>typical in many ways.</i>

3
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:05,593
<i>Quiet, conservative, church-going.</i>

4
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:10,151
But recently, Hastings
has been rocked with controversy.

5
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:12,550
That's when this book,
<i>Secret Confessions,</i>

6
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,472
was published a few weeks ago

7
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:18,553
and became an instant
national bestseller.

8
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:22,395
Not since Grace Metalious
wrote her thinly-disguised expos‚

9
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:25,677
of a small town called Peyton Place

10
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:29,594
has a book like this
caused such a furore.

11
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:32,991
This is the home of the author
of <i>Secret Confessions,</i>

12
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:34,832
Mary Ann Newton,

13
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,199
a widow whose first book
is a local sensation.

14
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:43,397
Miss Newton, I'm Carl Burke,
and we'd like to ask you

15
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:47,109
a few questions
about <i>Secret Confessions.</i>

16
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:50,512
Well,

17
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:53,478
I don't think I should
be talking to you.

18
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:58,351
You'll have to talk
to my publisher first.

19
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:01,474
If you'll excuse me.

20
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,714
This is the home of
Barbara Gleason,

21
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:08,235
who some say
bears a strong resemblance

22
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:12,951
to one of the three leading characters
in this steamy drama.

23
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:15,758
Mrs. Gleason, I'm Carl Burke,

24
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:19,595
and we'd like to ask you
a few questions about--

25
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:23,756
Hastings is the kind of town

26
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:26,514
where everybody
knows everybody else,

27
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,069
and you can still find a parking place
here on Main Street,

28
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:33,028
like right in front of
Mark Douglas' jewellery store.

29
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,909
Mark Douglas may very well be
portrayed in <i>Secret Confessions</i>

30
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:41,790
as a compulsive gambler, local rou‚.

31
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:53,395
<i>Dr. Phillip Eagen is a resident</i>
<i>at the local hospital,</i>

32
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:54,754
<i>and many raise the question/</i>

33
00:03:54,920 --> 00:03:59,471
<i>''ls he the model for the third leading</i>
<i>character in</i> Secret <i>Confessions?''</i>

34
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:04,111
<i>A young doctor caught stealing drugs</i>
<i>from his own hospital.</i>

35
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:07,150
<i>Dr. Eagen?</i>
<i>Dr. Eagen, I'm Carl Burke.</i>

36
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:09,515
<i>- I know who you are.</i>
<i>- I'd like to ask a few questions.</i>

37
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:10,954
<i>- Go right ahead.</i>
<i>- Could we stop?</i>

38
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:13,509
<i>- I'm sorry. I'm in kind of a hurry.</i>
<i>- Well, Dr. Eagen,</i>

39
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:16,513
<i>is the character Dr. Clark Laszlo</i>
<i>based on yourself?</i>

40
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:20,798
<i>No, it's not. Unfortunately,</i>
<i>a lot of people think otherwise.</i>

41
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:23,674
<i>Are you going to sue Mrs. Newton?</i>

42
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,031
<i>A lawsuit would be very expensive</i>
<i>and could take years.</i>

43
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,794
<i>Besides, it won't restore the damage</i>
<i>done to my medical career.</i>

44
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:32,757
<i>No, a lawsuit's too good</i>
<i>for Mary Ann Newton,</i>

45
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:34,353
<i>and someone ought to</i>
<i>repay her in kind</i>

46
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:37,512
<i>for the damage she's done to</i>
<i>this town and people like myself.</i>

47
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:40,877
<i>Mary Ann Newton is a coward</i>
<i>and a liar,</i>

48
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:43,235
<i>and should be deeply ashamed</i>
<i>of what she's done.</i>

49
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:46,790
<i>I wish she'd had the integrity</i>
<i>to refrain from this slander campaign.</i>

50
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:48,154
<i>Thank you, doctor.</i>

51
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:51,710
<i>Well, things seem to be</i>
<i>heating up here in Has--</i>

52
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:14,637
- Cliff.
- Mary Ann.

53
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,109
There are eggs
all over my car again.

54
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:20,909
- I will fill out a report.
- This is the third time.

55
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:23,671
Well, let's hope it's the last.

56
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:26,991
Say, the waitress in here,
the one with the:

57
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:30,914
''Bedroom eyes that always say yes,
no matter what the utterance.''

58
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:33,036
Is that Rosemary Sawyer?

59
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,590
I wanna know if you're gonna
provide me with protection.

60
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:41,197
Oh, Mary Ann, it's hard to believe
anybody means you any real harm.

61
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:45,714
I saw that Phillip Eagen
on TV this morning.

62
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:48,155
He was practically threatening.

63
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:50,880
Well, if and when at such time

64
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,474
as person or persons
known or unknown

65
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:55,392
attempt to cause you bodily harm,

66
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:58,313
I will personally conduct
a full-scale investigation.

67
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:00,710
Till then, I can't do anything.
So you go on home

68
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:02,598
and don't write any more dirty books.

69
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:04,955
You know, that is Marjorie Sawyer.

70
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:08,192
I'm gonna have to spend more time
down there at the Coffee Cup Caf‚.

71
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:29,472
Mary Ann? It's Reverend DeLuca.

72
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:34,552
Good evening.

73
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:39,074
- Oh, Reverend, come in.
- Thank you.

74
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:43,869
Are you all right?

75
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:47,828
Oh, well, the nerves
are a little frayed, I suppose.

76
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,992
Perhaps that book
should never have been written.

77
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:54,229
I'm worried about you, Mary Ann.

78
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:58,518
Yeah. Everything will
calm down eventually.

79
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:02,719
- How about a nice cup of tea?
- That'd be lovely.

80
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:00,079
- Good morning, Ben.
- Cliff.

81
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,595
- Come on in.
- Morning.

82
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:06,830
Guess you heard about the excitement
we've had down here.

83
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,198
Oh, yes. It's all over the TV
and the radio.

84
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:18,672
Well, that's not the way
I remember it.

85
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:21,511
It's your move.

86
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:24,793
Well, don't rush me,
it's been three months.

87
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:30,471
- So how's the investigation coming?
- Oh, we made an arrest.

88
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:34,599
- Young doctor.
- What you got on him?

89
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:40,118
Footprints outside the window
where the shot was fired match his,

90
00:08:40,280 --> 00:08:42,714
and the murder weapon
was found in his car.

91
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:45,276
Why do you think
he killed this reverend?

92
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:48,671
I don't think that the reverend
was the target.

93
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:51,076
Oh, you think
he was after the author.

94
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:54,275
Certainly appears that way.

95
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:58,558
- Why'd you wanna see me?
- Well,

96
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:02,429
got the doctor in custody.
He needs a good lawyer.

97
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:07,715
When did you start soliciting lawyers
for your suspects?

98
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:13,430
I don't think he did it, Ben.

99
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:16,269
How's that?

100
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:19,079
Oh, I've known Phil Eagen
since he was a boy.

101
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:22,437
I know how he was raised.
Just doesn't feel right.

102
00:09:23,240 --> 00:09:26,835
Well, he sure had reason to be mad,
the way she wrote him up in her book.

103
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:29,593
You read that book?

104
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:35,917
Well, somebody had it down
at the courthouse and I glanced at it.

105
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:43,198
Pretty racy stuff.

106
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:47,748
I gotta charge him, Ben.

107
00:09:57,520 --> 00:10:01,911
Well, I guess we could
finish this another time.

108
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,275
Hey, hey, the--

109
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:13,431
The waitress in that book,
would that be, uh...?

110
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:16,637
- Marjorie Sawyer?
- Yeah.

111
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:19,950
I sincerely doubt it.

112
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:32,394
- What are you in for?
- You.

113
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:33,788
- Lawyer?
- That's right.

114
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:35,075
Court appoint you?

115
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:36,719
No, I'm a friend of Chief Johnson's.

116
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:39,314
Oh, well, no offence,
but I need a top gun here.

117
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:43,552
- Well, who you got in mind?
- Well, there's Herman Wilkinson.

118
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:45,999
Herman's all right.

119
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:48,791
- He's not too good, huh?
- No, he's all right.

120
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:52,157
- How about Luther Jarvis?
- Luther can be good.

121
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:55,520
- He's in and out?
- No, he can be good.

122
00:10:56,800 --> 00:10:58,153
What about Ben Matlock?

123
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,275
- Did you do it?
- Hell, no.

124
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:03,954
- You got an alibi?
- I was home reading.

125
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:06,798
- Aware of the evidence?
- It's a frame.

126
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:10,316
Look, I'm a doctor. I'm supposed to
save lives, not take them.

127
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:13,712
- This lady's done you a lot of damage.
- Not as much as this will.

128
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:16,474
You never answered my question
about Ben Matlock.

129
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:20,155
- Sounds like his kind of case.
- I hear he's expensive.

130
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:21,914
He's flexible.

131
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:24,637
I think I'll try him.

132
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:27,109
Thanks for the advice.
Please, no offence.

133
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:29,111
Oh, I'm rarely offended.

134
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:32,756
- I'll tell Chief Johnson you've decided.
- Thanks.

135
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:34,239
I'm ready.

136
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:37,996
The waitress in that book,
that wouldn't be--?

137
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:39,434
Marjorie Sawyer.

138
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,355
Dr. Phillip Eagen
has no prior criminal record.

139
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:56,351
He is totally dedicated
to his medical work

140
00:11:56,520 --> 00:11:57,839
at Hastings Community Hospital,

141
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,117
and we respectfully submit
that he's no threat to society.

142
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:03,919
In fact, there's no
real danger of flight.

143
00:12:04,080 --> 00:12:07,550
Defence hopes that the court will
release him on his own recognizance.

144
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:10,555
Bail is set at $25,000.

145
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:13,310
Court will take a 1 5 minute recess.

146
00:12:16,600 --> 00:12:18,636
I thought I hired Ben Matlock.

147
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:21,394
- You did.
- Morning.

148
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:23,358
- You're Ben Matlock?
- Yeah.

149
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:26,956
I got a videotape from the TV station.
We'll go to the office and look at it.

150
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:28,553
Why didn't you tell me?

151
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:32,269
I didn't want to unduly influence you.
I'll post bail and then we'Il:

152
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:36,396
<i>I wish she'd had the integrity</i>
<i>to refrain from this slander campaign.</i>

153
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:38,391
<i>Thank you, doctor.</i>

154
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:43,358
So everybody thinks you're this
Dr. Laszlo in Miss Newton's book.

155
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:44,509
Of course they do.

156
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:47,194
The physical description fits
and the character was involved

157
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:49,590
in a theft of drugs from the hospital.

158
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:52,149
Did you read that book?

159
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:56,279
Well, yeah.
To prepare for the case.

160
00:12:58,560 --> 00:12:59,549
Were you?

161
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:01,992
- What?
- Involved in drug theft.

162
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:03,593
No formal charges were filed.

163
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:07,835
- Don't hedge with me.
- I didn't do it, another resident did.

164
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:09,911
Uppers, mostly,
to keep up with the hours.

165
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:11,479
I didn't turn him in.

166
00:13:53,320 --> 00:13:54,435
Who are you?

167
00:13:55,560 --> 00:14:00,350
Oh, I'm sorry. I'm Ben Matlock,
Phillip Eagen's lawyer.

168
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:04,829
What do you want?

169
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,992
I was just
looking through your window.

170
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:10,198
I noticed.

171
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:13,079
Well, since everybody assumes
you were the one who was shot at,

172
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:15,356
I was just trying to see
if I could figure out

173
00:14:15,520 --> 00:14:18,956
how whoever did it
could make that big a mistake.

174
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:20,829
And?

175
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:23,951
It's possible.

176
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:26,873
Is that where
they found the footprint?

177
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:29,998
Yes, I think so.

178
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:31,912
Well, can you think of why anybody

179
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:35,356
would wanna shoot
this Reverend DeLuca?

180
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:36,635
Mr. Matlock,

181
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:41,476
Reverend DeLuca is a man who
devoted his entire life to the Church.

182
00:14:42,440 --> 00:14:45,557
I can't imagine why anyone
would wanna do him any harm.

183
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:47,753
What was he doing here?

184
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:50,593
Well, he was concerned about me,

185
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:55,311
and he came to get a donation
I'd promised.

186
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:58,040
See, I pledged

187
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:03,228
to give a percentage
of my royalties to the Church.

188
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:05,072
Very generous.

189
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:09,119
Mr. Matlock, I didn't write this book

190
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:13,717
because I wanted to
stir up trouble or hurt anyone.

191
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:17,509
I wrote it because I wanted to
use a small town like this one

192
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:20,319
as a moral example.

193
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:23,712
It never occurred to you
that people might be upset?

194
00:15:25,880 --> 00:15:28,713
Never occurred to me
it would ever get published.

195
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:36,235
I not only dislike Mary Ann Newton,
Miss Matlock.

196
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:39,870
I hold her responsible
for the breakup of my marriage.

197
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:42,759
Because of the character
she patterned after you in her book?

198
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:46,391
A character that slept
with half a dozen men in her town.

199
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:49,677
- Try explaining that to a husband.
- Did you?

200
00:15:50,920 --> 00:15:54,833
Yes. But what was it to her?

201
00:15:56,440 --> 00:15:59,637
Mrs. Gleason, where were you
the night of the murder?

202
00:16:00,080 --> 00:16:02,992
- I was alone, why?
- I have to ask.

203
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:06,715
- What are you insinuating?
- Just part of my job.

204
00:16:06,880 --> 00:16:08,950
Asking, not insinuating.

205
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:15,914
That's a very lovely
flower arrangement.

206
00:16:17,720 --> 00:16:22,111
Look, my husband is the manager
of the bank in town.

207
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:25,791
He wasn't coming home
till 1 0 or 1 1 :00 at night.

208
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:27,109
I got bored.

209
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:30,989
I like TV, but night after night,
seven nights a week...

210
00:16:31,160 --> 00:16:36,439
But whatever I did, it was personal.
It was private, and she had no right.

211
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:40,036
Mary Ann Newton
is like a plague or worse.

212
00:16:41,720 --> 00:16:44,075
- Excuse me.
- Of course.

213
00:17:06,840 --> 00:17:08,239
Excuse me.

214
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:10,789
How much is this watch right here?

215
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:13,349
The round--? That's 200.

216
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:16,159
I remember when they used to
go for 20.

217
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:18,276
Maybe you should try the drugstore.

218
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:23,672
I guess Mary Ann Newton's book
didn't help business much.

219
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:24,989
What do you think?

220
00:17:25,640 --> 00:17:28,871
She writes about the only jeweller
in a small town

221
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:31,429
who winds up owing everybody
because of his gambling problem.

222
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:33,955
Now, I am the only jeweller
in this small town,

223
00:17:34,120 --> 00:17:37,157
so who else would everybody
think she was writing about?

224
00:17:37,920 --> 00:17:39,990
So business just dropped right off.

225
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:44,632
Let me put it this way,
you're my best customer.

226
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:49,279
Boy, being in a book like that
sure would make me mad.

227
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:50,429
You got it.

228
00:17:50,600 --> 00:17:53,876
I guess you'd like to
get back at Mary Ann Newton.

229
00:17:59,680 --> 00:18:01,079
Who the hell are you?

230
00:18:01,560 --> 00:18:02,709
Ben Matlock.

231
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:05,952
I represent Phillip Eagen
in this murder case.

232
00:18:06,120 --> 00:18:07,838
I don't suppose you'd remember

233
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,549
where you were
the night Reverend DeLuca was shot.

234
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:13,551
I'm not answering
any of your questions.

235
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:15,312
Well, you have to answer.

236
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:17,789
Either here or I can send you
a written invitation

237
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:22,636
to a beautifully-carved wooden chair
just a few feet from the judge.

238
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:25,788
I spent the whole night
with my girlfriend.

239
00:18:25,960 --> 00:18:28,190
Her name is Jessie Peters.

240
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:30,157
Now, please,
would you just leave the store?

241
00:18:30,320 --> 00:18:32,276
Oh, you got it. Thank you.
Appreciate it.

242
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:37,997
Keep an eye on him.

243
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,596
His girlfriend's named Jessie Peters.
He used her as an alibi.

244
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:49,673
What are you gonna do?

245
00:18:50,440 --> 00:18:54,319
I gotta go over to the Coffee Cup Caf‚
to see somebody,

246
00:18:54,480 --> 00:18:57,631
then I'll be over with Cliff Johnson
at the jailhouse.

247
00:19:19,520 --> 00:19:22,159
Hi, Mrs. Peters. I'm sorry I'm late.

248
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:25,197
- Who are you?
- Tyler Hudson.

249
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:28,670
- You did get my letter.
- I don't think so.

250
00:19:29,800 --> 00:19:32,439
Well, look, since I'm here,
I'd be glad to take a look around.

251
00:19:32,600 --> 00:19:34,511
- Look around what?
- Your house.

252
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:39,037
- You did want an appraisal.
- Oh, my house is not for sale.

253
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:43,030
Nobody's house on this block
was for sale

254
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:46,033
until they found out that the
expressway is coming through here.

255
00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:49,909
- I've never heard anything about that.
- See what I mean?

256
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:51,991
A lot of these people
are selling their houses now

257
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:55,709
before the official announcement
is made and the values go down.

258
00:19:57,200 --> 00:19:58,952
- How much is this gonna cost?
- No, no, no.

259
00:19:59,120 --> 00:20:00,348
This doesn't cost you anything.

260
00:20:00,520 --> 00:20:02,715
The Real Estate Association
will get my bill.

261
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:04,438
Look, let me help you with these.

262
00:20:04,600 --> 00:20:07,797
- Look, l--
- Come along.

263
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:17,473
- How many square feet do you have?
- You know that's for after lunch.

264
00:20:20,120 --> 00:20:22,873
I really don't know.

265
00:20:23,360 --> 00:20:26,113
Well, do you mind
if I take a little look around?

266
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:29,078
- Sure, go ahead.
- Hey, you know what?

267
00:20:29,240 --> 00:20:31,913
I bet you're gonna ask me
if I'd like to have a cup of tea.

268
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:40,639
- Yeah. Would you like a cup of tea?
- I would love a cup of tea.

269
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:41,915
I'm gonna take a look around.

270
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:43,513
- Okay.
- All right.

271
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:56,877
When are they gonna
make the announcement?

272
00:20:57,040 --> 00:20:59,679
- What announcement?
- The expressway.

273
00:21:00,280 --> 00:21:04,910
Oh, they're not sure, but soon.
Very, very soon.

274
00:21:06,080 --> 00:21:08,275
Oh, I'll be right back.

275
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:20,117
<i>Jessie, if you hear from a guy</i>
<i>named Matlock,</i>

276
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,236
<i>tell him I spent all night</i>
<i>Wednesday with you.</i>

277
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:23,799
<i>I'll explain later.</i>

278
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:31,036
Mr. Hudson?

279
00:21:35,320 --> 00:21:37,356
Mr. Hudson?

280
00:21:48,680 --> 00:21:51,831
<i>Jessie, if you hear from a guy</i>
<i>named Matlock,</i>

281
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:53,877
<i>tell him I spent all night</i>
<i>Wednesday with you.</i>

282
00:21:54,040 --> 00:21:56,395
<i>I'll explain later.</i>

283
00:22:09,280 --> 00:22:13,512
Let me speak to Mr. Douglas.
Well, where is he?

284
00:22:14,440 --> 00:22:16,351
Would you tell him to call me as soon
as possible?

285
00:22:16,520 --> 00:22:18,511
It's very important.

286
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:36,394
What are you doing here?

287
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:38,952
The florist gave me directions.

288
00:22:39,920 --> 00:22:44,710
The man who sent you flowers. Bill.
One of your gentleman friends.

289
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:46,029
You read the note?

290
00:22:46,560 --> 00:22:47,879
Is that Bill over there?

291
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:52,909
He sent me flowers.
He's very thoughtful.

292
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:55,036
But is he the last
of the red-hot lovers?

293
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:58,513
Barbara, you've been working here

294
00:22:58,680 --> 00:23:01,956
three nights a week
for the past 1 8 months.

295
00:23:02,840 --> 00:23:05,877
This is very honourable,
but you lied to me.

296
00:23:06,040 --> 00:23:08,156
You gave me some long story
about how you were at home

297
00:23:08,320 --> 00:23:09,753
and Ionely all the time.

298
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:11,754
I have my reasons.

299
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:14,673
But you weren't at home
and you weren't bored.

300
00:23:14,840 --> 00:23:18,469
It's almost as though you wanted to
have people think you're having affairs.

301
00:23:19,800 --> 00:23:21,791
Could we talk outside?

302
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:36,634
- This stays between us, okay?
- What if I need it in court?

303
00:23:36,800 --> 00:23:39,075
I don't think you would.

304
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:43,116
Everything I told you
about my husband was true.

305
00:23:43,280 --> 00:23:44,429
I tried everything.

306
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:49,549
I lost weight, I gained weight,
I used makeup, no makeup.

307
00:23:49,720 --> 00:23:51,995
I had black hair, I dyed it red.

308
00:23:52,160 --> 00:23:54,355
I walked around with clothes on,

309
00:23:54,520 --> 00:23:58,513
I walked around with some clothes on,
I walked around with no clothes on.

310
00:23:58,680 --> 00:24:00,636
He was just losing interest in me.

311
00:24:00,800 --> 00:24:02,711
Well, I certainly
don't understand that.

312
00:24:03,560 --> 00:24:05,630
But it happened,

313
00:24:05,800 --> 00:24:09,873
so I decided to try
and make him jealous.

314
00:24:10,040 --> 00:24:13,919
I know it's stupid, but what the hell?

315
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:17,395
So you mean you weren't
really having any...?

316
00:24:17,560 --> 00:24:23,510
I tried. I mean, I really tried,
but it's a small town and...

317
00:24:24,280 --> 00:24:26,316
So, Barbara, you weren't very upset

318
00:24:26,480 --> 00:24:29,278
about Mary Ann writing about you
in her book?

319
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:32,389
No.

320
00:24:34,440 --> 00:24:37,591
I've become a very desirable woman.

321
00:24:41,360 --> 00:24:46,992
I show you People's Exhibit 6
and I ask if you recognise it.

322
00:24:47,160 --> 00:24:48,639
Yes, sir.

323
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:52,076
We found that weapon in the glove
compartment of Phillip Eagen's car.

324
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:54,708
And the Hastings Police Department
ballistics laboratory

325
00:24:54,880 --> 00:24:56,393
performed tests on this weapon?

326
00:24:56,560 --> 00:25:00,314
Why, sir, I am the Hastings Police
Department Ballistics Department.

327
00:25:00,800 --> 00:25:05,191
- Yes, I did.
- Tell us the results of the tests.

328
00:25:06,160 --> 00:25:08,116
In my opinion,

329
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:11,590
that's the weapon
that killed Reverend DeLuca.

330
00:25:13,360 --> 00:25:16,636
And were any other physical evidence
secured from the scene of the crime?

331
00:25:17,680 --> 00:25:19,716
The angle and trajectory of the shots

332
00:25:19,880 --> 00:25:22,872
indicated that they were fired
through the side window.

333
00:25:23,040 --> 00:25:24,029
Outside that window,

334
00:25:24,200 --> 00:25:27,351
we found some freshly-made footprints
in the flowerbed.

335
00:25:27,520 --> 00:25:29,750
What did you ascertain
about those footprints?

336
00:25:29,920 --> 00:25:33,356
That they were made
by a man, size 1 2 shoe.

337
00:25:33,520 --> 00:25:37,798
- The same size as the defendant's.
- Yes, sir.

338
00:25:38,360 --> 00:25:40,271
No further questions.
Thank you, chief.

339
00:25:40,440 --> 00:25:42,271
Cross-examine?

340
00:25:45,480 --> 00:25:47,391
You know, chief,

341
00:25:47,920 --> 00:25:53,153
I'm impressed with the forensics work
police departments do nowadays.

342
00:25:53,320 --> 00:25:55,470
They can find a thread of hair

343
00:25:55,640 --> 00:25:58,677
and they can tell you the colour
of the person's hair,

344
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:02,753
how old he is,
when he had his last haircut,

345
00:26:02,920 --> 00:26:04,638
probably what he had for breakfast.

346
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:07,680
Not quite what he had for breakfast.

347
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:10,317
Well, what if...?

348
00:26:10,680 --> 00:26:16,596
What if I made a footprint
in this box of sand?

349
00:26:18,320 --> 00:26:22,871
Could you tell me from that footprint
what my shoe size is?

350
00:26:23,040 --> 00:26:26,874
Well, surely. I'd need a tape measure
and a scrap of paper.

351
00:26:27,520 --> 00:26:32,355
Okay. There you go. There.

352
00:26:32,760 --> 00:26:34,990
And here I go.

353
00:26:35,960 --> 00:26:37,791
Let's see.

354
00:26:42,720 --> 00:26:46,076
Okay, that's it.

355
00:26:54,600 --> 00:26:56,192
Now, this is not exact,

356
00:26:57,320 --> 00:27:02,314
but you are wearing a size 1 2 shoe,
probably leather-soled.

357
00:27:02,480 --> 00:27:07,554
The shoe I'm wearing does have a
leather sole and it's exactly a size 1 2.

358
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:12,511
But my foot size is a 1 0.

359
00:27:12,920 --> 00:27:17,550
So, what I did, I shoved cotton in there
to make up the difference.

360
00:27:17,720 --> 00:27:20,757
Now, chief, the only thing we know

361
00:27:20,920 --> 00:27:24,117
about the person we believe
shot through that window

362
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:26,236
is that he wore a size 1 2 shoe.
Is that right?

363
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:27,389
That's right.

364
00:27:27,560 --> 00:27:31,030
So it could be that somebody
other than Phillip Eagen

365
00:27:31,200 --> 00:27:33,634
did what I just did
to throw off the police.

366
00:27:33,800 --> 00:27:36,598
- Is that right?
- That's right.

367
00:27:37,160 --> 00:27:40,675
Thank you, chief.
No further questions.

368
00:27:41,960 --> 00:27:43,075
Redirect?

369
00:27:43,760 --> 00:27:45,193
No, Your Honour.

370
00:27:45,360 --> 00:27:49,114
This court will be in recess
until 9 a.m. tomorrow.

371
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:51,236
I remind the jury
that they are admonished

372
00:27:51,400 --> 00:27:56,190
not to discuss this case,
nor form any opinions.

373
00:27:59,080 --> 00:28:00,991
Miss Peters?

374
00:28:02,200 --> 00:28:05,078
Would you step outside here
with me just a second?

375
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:17,556
What do you want?

376
00:28:17,880 --> 00:28:20,348
Mark Daniels was not with you
the night of the murder.

377
00:28:20,520 --> 00:28:22,511
Can you prove that?

378
00:28:23,240 --> 00:28:26,277
My associate tells me
that you have an 8-year-old son,

379
00:28:26,440 --> 00:28:28,192
and that you have an ex-husband
in California

380
00:28:28,360 --> 00:28:30,112
who wants custody of the boy.

381
00:28:30,280 --> 00:28:33,636
Now, I'm not trying to blackmail you
or intimidate you,

382
00:28:33,800 --> 00:28:38,669
but if you commit perjury on the stand,
I have no choice but to go after you,

383
00:28:38,840 --> 00:28:41,354
and that could provide
your ex-husband with ammunition

384
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:44,034
in any custody battle.

385
00:28:45,440 --> 00:28:49,194
- What do you want me to do?
- Well...

386
00:28:49,800 --> 00:28:52,997
Now, Mark Douglas has brought you
into this to do him a favour,

387
00:28:53,200 --> 00:28:55,350
and I think you'd be much
better advised

388
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:57,909
to do yourself a favour
and cooperate with me.

389
00:28:58,080 --> 00:28:59,832
Do you think Mark did it?

390
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,719
I think Mark
has a lot of explaining to do.

391
00:29:07,640 --> 00:29:10,996
If I tell you what you wanna know,
do I have to testify?

392
00:29:12,800 --> 00:29:14,313
Maybe not.

393
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:16,712
Mark was with a guy
Wednesday night.

394
00:29:16,880 --> 00:29:18,074
Some kind of business.

395
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:24,832
- What's the big secret?
- I don't know, but it was a big secret.

396
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:29,755
He was a guy from Atlanta.

397
00:29:30,520 --> 00:29:32,715
Funny-sounding name.

398
00:29:38,160 --> 00:29:40,469
- Hey, Tyler?
- Yeah?

399
00:29:43,520 --> 00:29:46,193
Some guy from Atlanta,
maybe deals in jewellery,

400
00:29:46,360 --> 00:29:47,952
maybe hot, funny-sounding name.

401
00:29:48,120 --> 00:29:51,157
- Schimmelburger.
- Funnier.

402
00:29:51,320 --> 00:29:53,276
- Grock.
- Grock.

403
00:29:55,440 --> 00:29:56,429
Grock.

404
00:29:58,760 --> 00:30:00,193
You were supposed to sell this stuff.

405
00:30:00,360 --> 00:30:03,397
I will. Things are a little tight right now,
so just take it away

406
00:30:03,560 --> 00:30:05,630
for a couple of weeks,
and then when things cool down,

407
00:30:05,800 --> 00:30:07,950
it'll be business as usual.

408
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:16,754
- Who's this?
- I don't know. A customer, I hope.

409
00:30:21,680 --> 00:30:24,592
Hey, come on, open up. Look, I wanna
match a ring with this watch.

410
00:30:24,760 --> 00:30:28,514
- No, it's too early to open right now.
- I can't hear what you're saying.

411
00:30:28,680 --> 00:30:31,148
It'll be about an hour and--
What are you--? What--?

412
00:30:31,320 --> 00:30:33,880
- Hello, Grock.
- What the hell is this?

413
00:30:34,040 --> 00:30:37,476
We're not here in any official capacity,
Mr. Grock.

414
00:30:37,640 --> 00:30:39,119
- You're not?
- No.

415
00:30:39,280 --> 00:30:41,874
All we're after is some information
on your associate here

416
00:30:42,040 --> 00:30:44,031
and where he was
last Wednesday night.

417
00:30:44,200 --> 00:30:46,509
Look, you have no right
to come barging into this shop.

418
00:30:46,680 --> 00:30:48,750
Shut up, Mark. I'm a businessman.

419
00:30:48,920 --> 00:30:50,399
We know
what your business is, Grock.

420
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:54,473
All we wanna know is were you
and Mr. Douglas doing business

421
00:30:54,640 --> 00:30:57,916
last Wednesday night
while a murder was being committed?

422
00:30:58,400 --> 00:30:59,879
Look, Mr. Matlock--

423
00:31:00,920 --> 00:31:02,911
You have a reputation too,
you know.

424
00:31:03,120 --> 00:31:05,839
--maybe I move a few pieces
of jewellery from time to time,

425
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:07,399
but I am not in on a murder ticket.

426
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:11,876
Oh, that's why you faked your alibi.

427
00:31:12,040 --> 00:31:15,316
Because at the time
Reverend DeLuca was murdered,

428
00:31:15,480 --> 00:31:19,234
you and Mr. Grock here
were trafficking in stolen merchandise.

429
00:31:19,400 --> 00:31:20,879
What did you want me to do?

430
00:31:21,040 --> 00:31:23,508
Come out, tell you what I was doing
so I could go to jail?

431
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:25,989
Are we supposed to believe this?

432
00:31:26,160 --> 00:31:29,516
Well, he made up an alibi
that backfired.

433
00:31:30,880 --> 00:31:35,112
You will go to jail, but you won't
go to jail for murder.

434
00:31:35,280 --> 00:31:38,272
Congratulations, and to you,
Mr. Grock.

435
00:31:55,960 --> 00:31:59,077
You two look terrible.
What happened in there?

436
00:31:59,240 --> 00:32:02,710
Douglas is guilty of a crime,
but it's not the right crime.

437
00:32:05,440 --> 00:32:06,668
Well...

438
00:32:06,840 --> 00:32:10,515
If Douglas didn't commit the murder,
and neither did Barbara Gleason,

439
00:32:10,680 --> 00:32:12,318
who did?

440
00:32:13,320 --> 00:32:18,314
Well, as far as I can tell, Charlene,

441
00:32:18,920 --> 00:32:20,592
nobody.

442
00:32:33,840 --> 00:32:39,517
I hear you've been hanging around
the Coffee Cup Caf‚.

443
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:46,193
Well, a person
has to eat somewhere.

444
00:32:51,240 --> 00:32:55,711
You going to subpoena
Marjorie Sawyer?

445
00:32:56,800 --> 00:32:58,074
Who?

446
00:32:59,360 --> 00:33:01,237
You know who.

447
00:33:02,800 --> 00:33:04,074
I might.

448
00:33:08,640 --> 00:33:13,395
Yeah, you get old Marjorie Sawyer
over there in Atlanta,

449
00:33:14,040 --> 00:33:17,715
and the court will let out,
it'll be late,

450
00:33:18,240 --> 00:33:20,993
she won't have anyplace to stay,

451
00:33:21,160 --> 00:33:26,757
and you'll be standing there
like ready money.

452
00:33:32,920 --> 00:33:37,869
You know, police work has given you
an unduly suspicious nature.

453
00:33:41,080 --> 00:33:42,354
What say, Tyler? How's it going?

454
00:33:42,520 --> 00:33:44,238
- Ben, I've been snooping.
- Snoop, huh?

455
00:33:44,400 --> 00:33:47,278
I snooped around Reverend DeLuca's
office over at the rectory,

456
00:33:47,440 --> 00:33:49,431
and look what I found.

457
00:33:51,680 --> 00:33:56,800
- Well, I'll be.
- Ain't that something?

458
00:34:02,560 --> 00:34:05,120
- Hang on to that.
- Sure will.

459
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:09,150
- What's that mean?
- I don't know yet.

460
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:15,192
King me.

461
00:34:21,360 --> 00:34:24,557
Mr. Sears? Mr. Sears?

462
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:28,391
Ben Matlock.

463
00:34:28,560 --> 00:34:31,757
- You mind if I walk along?
- Help yourself.

464
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:34,718
- Nice dog.
- I know.

465
00:34:34,880 --> 00:34:36,472
The neighbours don't mind
all this ruckus?

466
00:34:36,640 --> 00:34:37,959
Oh, the hell with them.

467
00:34:38,120 --> 00:34:41,396
You walk your dog
every night at this time?

468
00:34:41,560 --> 00:34:44,518
I like to get back for Johnny Carson.

469
00:35:11,880 --> 00:35:14,838
Oh, sorry.

470
00:35:16,640 --> 00:35:19,393
Go around.

471
00:35:29,080 --> 00:35:32,595
I'm sorry to surprise you,
but tomorrow's the big day,

472
00:35:32,760 --> 00:35:35,194
and I'd like to go over the events
of the night of the murder

473
00:35:35,360 --> 00:35:36,918
just one last time.

474
00:35:37,080 --> 00:35:39,071
- Oh, sure.
- Thank you.

475
00:35:39,240 --> 00:35:41,071
Now, let's see.

476
00:35:43,320 --> 00:35:50,112
You came in from the kitchen
and saw the body from about here.

477
00:35:50,280 --> 00:35:51,269
That's right.

478
00:35:51,440 --> 00:35:53,078
Then you dropped the tray
and screamed

479
00:35:53,240 --> 00:35:56,869
when you saw Mr. Sears
staring at you through the window.

480
00:35:57,840 --> 00:36:00,035
- That's right.
- Okay.

481
00:36:01,240 --> 00:36:02,832
Well, thank you.

482
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:06,629
And, again, I'm sorry to interrupt.

483
00:36:07,800 --> 00:36:10,473
If I were to call you to the stand
over in Atlanta,

484
00:36:10,640 --> 00:36:13,154
would you tell me this again there?

485
00:36:13,320 --> 00:36:16,278
- Oh, sure.
- Thank you.

486
00:36:20,960 --> 00:36:25,875
Miss Newton, I've got a little
confession to make.

487
00:36:26,040 --> 00:36:30,397
I was apprehensive
about reading your book.

488
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:32,915
I don't know,
I guess I'm old-fashioned.

489
00:36:33,080 --> 00:36:35,310
There's some pretty
racy stuff in there.

490
00:36:35,480 --> 00:36:40,759
Yes, I know, but it has redeeming
social value.

491
00:36:41,280 --> 00:36:45,273
Well, to tell the truth, I did read it
and I found it very engrossing.

492
00:36:45,440 --> 00:36:46,668
Oh, thank you.

493
00:36:48,080 --> 00:36:51,038
When you write,
do you write on a typewriter?

494
00:36:51,200 --> 00:36:53,430
No, I use a word processor.

495
00:36:55,600 --> 00:37:00,628
I don't know much about these
electronic typing machines.

496
00:37:00,800 --> 00:37:04,236
What, do you just type it all in there
and then punch a few buttons

497
00:37:04,400 --> 00:37:06,470
and the machine prints it up?
Is that about it?

498
00:37:07,080 --> 00:37:08,479
That's about it.

499
00:37:09,360 --> 00:37:11,999
And that's how you wrote
<i>Secret Confessions?</i>

500
00:37:12,480 --> 00:37:15,199
Yes, it is, Mr. Matlock.

501
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:20,118
Have you ever read
<i>Murder is my Calling?</i>

502
00:37:20,280 --> 00:37:21,269
No.

503
00:37:21,440 --> 00:37:24,477
How about
<i>Now I Lay Her Down to Sleep?</i>

504
00:37:24,640 --> 00:37:26,392
I don't think so.

505
00:37:26,560 --> 00:37:29,870
Did you read
<i>Valley of the Shadow of Death,</i>

506
00:37:30,040 --> 00:37:32,998
or <i>See No Evil,</i> or <i>Adam's Serpent?</i>

507
00:37:33,680 --> 00:37:34,954
No, I didn't.

508
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:40,438
Neither did I,
and I'm sure nobody else did

509
00:37:40,600 --> 00:37:44,229
except the publisher
who rejected them.

510
00:37:44,400 --> 00:37:46,072
You know who wrote these?

511
00:37:47,760 --> 00:37:49,273
I'm afraid I don't.

512
00:37:50,840 --> 00:37:53,877
They were all written
by the same person.

513
00:37:54,280 --> 00:37:56,157
Reverend DeLuca.

514
00:37:59,400 --> 00:38:04,554
And they were all rejected
by these publishers.

515
00:38:08,320 --> 00:38:11,437
Do you--? Thank you.

516
00:38:12,800 --> 00:38:16,509
Do you recognise this typewriter?

517
00:38:18,520 --> 00:38:23,435
- No, I don't think I do.
- It belonged to Reverend DeLuca.

518
00:38:23,600 --> 00:38:28,469
And I'd like this typewriter marked
Defendant Exhibit A,

519
00:38:28,960 --> 00:38:33,829
and I'd like this manuscript
marked Defence Exhibit B.

520
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:35,115
So marked.

521
00:38:35,840 --> 00:38:38,115
<i>Secret Confessions,</i>

522
00:38:38,440 --> 00:38:41,079
and it was not written
on a word processor.

523
00:38:41,240 --> 00:38:45,233
It was written on this typewriter
by Reverend DeLuca.

524
00:38:45,400 --> 00:38:46,879
Objection, Your Honour.

525
00:38:47,040 --> 00:38:49,110
Whether or not Miss Newton
wrote <i>Secret Confessions</i>

526
00:38:49,280 --> 00:38:51,840
is irrelevant to this case.

527
00:38:53,240 --> 00:38:56,357
It is, Your Honour,
if it shows motive.

528
00:38:56,960 --> 00:38:59,394
- Overruled.
- Thank you, Your Honour.

529
00:38:59,560 --> 00:39:03,155
Miss Newton, Reverend DeLuca
was a very frustrated writer.

530
00:39:03,320 --> 00:39:07,950
He wrote one mystery after another
and nobody would buy them.

531
00:39:08,840 --> 00:39:10,512
Then he wrote this novel,

532
00:39:10,680 --> 00:39:13,558
but he knew if he sent it in
with his name on it,

533
00:39:13,720 --> 00:39:15,472
it probably wouldn't get published.

534
00:39:15,640 --> 00:39:17,551
Just like the others
didn't get published.

535
00:39:18,360 --> 00:39:21,670
Besides, it was pretty seamy stuff
for a preacher.

536
00:39:21,840 --> 00:39:25,753
And so you and he
came up with the idea:

537
00:39:25,920 --> 00:39:32,268
What if it were written by a little
spinster lady in a small town?

538
00:39:35,560 --> 00:39:37,630
Might get published.

539
00:39:38,160 --> 00:39:42,870
People might find it titillating,
and, in fact, they did.

540
00:39:45,640 --> 00:39:50,031
Now, isn't that why the two of you
put your name on it?

541
00:39:52,200 --> 00:39:54,111
I wrote it.

542
00:39:58,360 --> 00:40:02,956
You know, Miss Newton,
we all have secrets,

543
00:40:03,400 --> 00:40:06,631
and I think yours is that
you came

544
00:40:06,800 --> 00:40:11,669
from being a little spinster lady
in a little town in Georgia

545
00:40:11,840 --> 00:40:16,516
to becoming a big spinster lady
in a little town in Georgia in a big way.

546
00:40:16,680 --> 00:40:22,835
Gee, you're on television, on radio.
People are writing about you.

547
00:40:23,000 --> 00:40:26,754
People recognise you on the street,
and you kind of like that.

548
00:40:26,920 --> 00:40:29,514
Some of them got mad, but...

549
00:40:30,440 --> 00:40:33,512
And you know,
I think it got away from you.

550
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:40,958
I think somewhere in here
it got away from you

551
00:40:41,960 --> 00:40:47,318
to the point that you never
wanted anybody to know your secret.

552
00:40:48,960 --> 00:40:50,791
The thing that made you so big,

553
00:40:52,040 --> 00:40:54,190
the reason why we're here.

554
00:40:55,120 --> 00:40:57,156
That's it, isn't it?

555
00:40:57,320 --> 00:41:02,394
That you did not want to share
your book with Reverend DeLuca?

556
00:41:02,560 --> 00:41:06,394
- Objection, Your Honour.
- I'll withdraw that question.

557
00:41:08,120 --> 00:41:10,031
Let me...

558
00:41:11,920 --> 00:41:17,313
Let me show it to you
like it was a chapter in your book.

559
00:41:18,480 --> 00:41:21,517
You asked Reverend DeLuca
to come over to your house

560
00:41:21,680 --> 00:41:24,592
a few minutes before 1 1 .
Isn't that right?

561
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:26,269
Well, yes, but--

562
00:41:26,440 --> 00:41:31,514
And you knew that David Sears
walks his dog every night

563
00:41:31,680 --> 00:41:33,511
a few minutes after 1 1 .

564
00:41:33,680 --> 00:41:38,390
So you waited till you heard
the other dogs in the block barking,

565
00:41:39,440 --> 00:41:43,558
and you asked Reverend DeLuca
if he'd like some tea.

566
00:41:43,720 --> 00:41:45,312
He would.

567
00:41:47,320 --> 00:41:51,711
You went outside, around the house,

568
00:41:52,440 --> 00:41:57,468
to the side window,
where you shot Reverend DeLuca.

569
00:41:58,040 --> 00:42:00,110
I did not!

570
00:42:00,280 --> 00:42:03,352
You know Mr. Sears is elderly
and can't walk fast,

571
00:42:03,960 --> 00:42:08,238
so you had plenty of time
to go back into the house,

572
00:42:08,760 --> 00:42:13,595
pick up the tea tray, walk in,
drop it and look shocked

573
00:42:13,760 --> 00:42:16,149
when Mr. Sears came up
and looked through your window.

574
00:42:16,320 --> 00:42:18,197
That's not true.

575
00:42:21,600 --> 00:42:23,556
I think it is.

576
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:27,874
I think after you shot
Reverend DeLuca

577
00:42:28,600 --> 00:42:31,831
and before the police started
their full-scale investigation,

578
00:42:32,000 --> 00:42:35,993
you planted the gun in
Phillip Eagen's car perfectly.

579
00:42:36,160 --> 00:42:38,799
You knew he was mad
about the book anyway.

580
00:42:39,920 --> 00:42:41,876
Phillip Eagen
wears a size 1 2 shoe.

581
00:42:42,040 --> 00:42:46,238
You found that out, got a size 1 2 shoe,
made the footprint.

582
00:42:47,760 --> 00:42:50,877
And in an effort
to be truly convincing,

583
00:42:51,440 --> 00:42:53,829
you dropped the tea tray

584
00:42:54,000 --> 00:42:56,798
when you saw Mr. Sears
looking through the window.

585
00:42:56,960 --> 00:42:59,872
But you know, last night
when I was over there,

586
00:43:00,040 --> 00:43:02,156
there was something missing.

587
00:43:02,680 --> 00:43:06,753
It was missing from before,
but I hadn't put it together.

588
00:43:06,920 --> 00:43:08,319
You know what was missing?

589
00:43:11,920 --> 00:43:13,717
Tea stains.

590
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:17,470
There were no tea stains on the rug.
You know why?

591
00:43:21,360 --> 00:43:25,194
You didn't make any tea.
You didn't have time.

592
00:43:25,360 --> 00:43:27,954
You didn't even have time
to boil water.

593
00:43:29,040 --> 00:43:33,989
That's not true.
You can't prove any of it.

594
00:43:37,920 --> 00:43:40,036
I don't have to.

595
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:45,998
All I have to do is show this jury
there is reasonable doubt

596
00:43:46,160 --> 00:43:50,153
that Phillip Eagen
shot Reverend DeLuca.

597
00:43:52,240 --> 00:43:55,789
It's my book. I wrote it.

598
00:43:58,120 --> 00:44:00,190
I'm sorry, Miss Newton.

599
00:44:02,200 --> 00:44:04,509
I guess that's all, Your Honour.

600
00:44:08,520 --> 00:44:10,715
It's my book.

601
00:44:13,720 --> 00:44:15,995
It's my book.

602
00:44:20,080 --> 00:44:22,310
It's my book.

603
00:44:26,080 --> 00:44:29,470
We find the defendant not guilty.

604
00:44:29,640 --> 00:44:32,154
Well, congratulations.

605
00:44:50,360 --> 00:44:53,989
- Enjoy the trial, Marjorie?
- Oh, it was very exciting.

606
00:44:54,160 --> 00:44:56,276
I'm sorry I didn't get to call you
as a witness.

607
00:44:56,440 --> 00:44:58,829
That's okay.

608
00:45:01,040 --> 00:45:05,397
It's getting late. The last bus
for Hastings left at 4:00.

609
00:45:06,840 --> 00:45:08,159
Good thing I hung around.

610
00:45:08,320 --> 00:45:09,753
Looks like Marjorie
needs a ride home.

611
00:45:09,920 --> 00:45:12,354
Say, next time you're down
around our part of the town,

612
00:45:12,520 --> 00:45:15,273
we'll finish that game, Ben.
See you later.

613
00:45:20,600 --> 00:45:23,512
- How about some dinner?
- Our treat.

614
00:45:23,680 --> 00:45:25,033
Yeah.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
